> My opinionated design:
> 
> 1. **Cite source excerpts, not whole artifacts.**  
>    Artifact IDs are useful provenance containers, but claims should ideally point to the exact `SourceExcerpt` or a snippet within a `web_source` artifact. Whole-page/artifact citations are too coarse.
> 
> 2. **Give the synthesis model citation handles in the evidence brief.**  
>    Right now `_execution_evidence_for_llm()` intentionally discards artifact IDs. I’d change that. Example evidence item:
> 
>    ```text
>    [S3]
>    Title: IPCC AR6 Synthesis Report
>    URL: https://...
>    Excerpt: Global surface temperature was 1.1°C higher in 2011–2020...
>    Artifact ID: ...
>    Source excerpt ID: ...
>    ```
> 
> 3. **Require inline citation handles for externally grounded claims.**  
>    In the final synthesis prompt:
> 
>    > For every factual claim based on retrieved evidence, cite one or more handles like `[[S3]]`. Do not invent handles. If no handle supports a claim, qualify it or omit it.
> 
> 4. **Post-process citations into a reference list.**  
>    Let the model write:
> 
>    ```markdown
>    Global surface temperature was about 1.1°C higher in 2011–2020 than 1850–1900 [[S3]].
>    ```
> 
>    Then your system parses `[[S3]]`, validates against allowed handles, replaces or keeps them, and appends:
> 
>    ```markdown
>    References
> 
>    [S3] IPCC AR6 Synthesis Report. https://...
>         Excerpt: “Global surface temperature was 1.1°C higher...”
>    ```
> 
> 5. **Persist structured citation metadata.**  
>    Add something like:
> 
>    ```python
>    @dataclass(frozen=True)
>    class AnswerCitation:
>        handle: str
>        artifact_id: str | None
>        source_excerpt_id: str | None
>        title: str | None
>        url: str | None
>        excerpt: str | None
>        used_in_response_id: str
>    ```
> 
> 6. **Validate citations before final delivery.**  
>    If the model cites `[[S99]]` and no such handle exists, either:
>    - rerun synthesis with an error message, or
>    - strip/flag unsupported claims.
> 
> 7. **Keep artifact IDs in `explain()`, not necessarily visible answer.**  
>    Users want meaningful references; you want auditability. So show title/URL/excerpt in the answer, and expose artifact/source IDs through `planner.explain(response_id)`.
> 
> Concretely, I’d introduce a `CitationResolver` between JavaScript execution and final synthesis:
> 
> ```text
> ToolResult/Artifacts/SourceExcerpts
>         ↓
> CitationResolver assigns S1, S2, S3...
>         ↓
> Evidence brief includes handles
>         ↓
> Model cites [[S1]]
>         ↓
> ReferenceRenderer validates + appends references
>         ↓
> ResponseRecord stores citation map
> ```
> 
> This gives you per-statement grounding without making the prose ugly or leaking implementation IDs as the main UX. Artifact IDs should be the backend spine; citation handles should be the model-facing and optionally user-facing layer.
